var textArgumentInjectionOption = lint.FindingText{ID: kindArgumentInjectionOption, Detected: "{command} gets a word that starts with the value of {expr_ref}, or, when the value is outside quotes or is a ${{ }} that the runner writes into the script before the shell reads the script, a word that starts after a blank in the value; an attacker controls the value of {expr_ref} on {controlled_on}; the word is at a place in the order of the words of {command} where {command} reads a word that starts with - as an option (a place before a word such as -- that ends the options), and the attacker can write at that place an option in the list {options}, which makes {command} run a program or code that is written after the name of the option", Consequence: "the attacker makes the attacker's value start with the name of one of the options that the finding names, followed by a program; the command reads the word as the option whose name starts the word, and runs the program in the step, with whatever the step can reach, such as the secrets and the token in its env, the credentials actions/checkout leaves in .git/config, the network, and sudo on a GitHub-hosted runner", Remedy: "A variable of env and double quotes (\"$X\") stop the shell from running the value as code, but the variable and the quotes do not stop the command from reading the value as an option. In either fix below, keep the value in the variable of env, and pass the variable to the command inside double quotes. Then either put the word that ends the command's options (--; --end-of-options for git) after the last option the command needs and before the value, for example right before the value (tar -xf archive.tar notes.txt -- \"$X\"), so that the command reads every word after that word as a plain argument; or, before the command, end the step with an error when the value starts with -, such as [[ $X != -* ]] || exit 1 for the variable X that holds the value. The shell check [[ $X != -* ]] works only when the command gets the value inside double quotes: when the command gets the value outside quotes, a blank in the value starts a new word, and the new word can start with -. For make, -- is not enough: make reads a word of the form NAME=value as the assignment of a variable even after --, so for make end the step with an error when the value starts with - or contains =, such as [[ $X != -* && $X != *=* ]] || exit 1", Arguments: []lint.KindArgument{{Name: lint.MustKindArgumentName("command"), Text: "The command and the subcommands of the command, such as tar or git fetch.", InDetected: true}, {Name: lint.MustKindArgumentName("expr_ref"), Text: "The reference whose value begins the word, such as github.event.pull_request.title.", InDetected: true}, {Name: lint.MustKindArgumentName("controlled_on"), Text: "The events on which an attacker controls the value of the expression, each event named once.", InDetected: true}, {Name: lint.MustKindArgumentName("options"), Text: "The options of the command that run a program, as the attacker writes the options: for one word that holds both the option and the program, a long option with = (--to-command=) or a short option followed by the program in the same word (-e<script>); for two words, the option and then the program, a short option as a word by itself (-c).", InDetected: true}}}

// ArgumentInjectionOption is the finding argument-injection.option: A value an attacker controls begins a word a command of a run gets, where the command reads a word that begins with - as an option, and the command has an option seen to run a program written in the attacker's spelling.
